  #line-at(70.824pt, 404.21pt, "employed in our simulations of the permeation process (Fig. 5d). Summarizing it appears, that ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 420.05pt, "the EC-modelling with the decay concept extension, along with the simplified forms of the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 435.77pt, "model, provide decent tools for the permeation data analyses.   ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(106.82pt, 459.91pt, "4.3")
  #line-at(124.82pt, 459.91pt, "Two-beams DRPA and the interface sorption kinetics ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 483.67pt, "In many practical cases involving mass transport between two phases, the role of the interface ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 499.51pt, "is reduced to that of a mathematical object: a null-thick boundary between subsystems of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 515.47pt, "different characteristics (e.g. diffusion coefficient, mass transfer coefficient, etc.), where ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 531.31pt, "additional constraints or conditions like flux continuity are imposed on the transport equations. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 547.15pt, "On the other hand, it is known that the physics of the interfaces is far more complex than a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 562.99pt, "simple step-wise variation of physico-chemical properties, and includes thermodynamical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 578.83pt, "definitions and concepts on the rheological effects and relaxation processes over a finite yet ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 594.79pt, "non-zero thick interfacial layer [27,28]. As such it is possible to differentiate between the bulk ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 610.63pt, "mass transport and the interfacial mass transfer rate influenced by both the interface ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 626.47pt, "equilibration kinetics and mass transfer coefficient. The transport parameters are related via the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 642.58pt, "transport rate number: ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(192.38pt, 642.58pt, "𝑁")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(200.21pt, 644.98pt, "𝑅")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(209.81pt, 642.58pt, "= 𝜏")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(227.69pt, 644.98pt, "𝑏𝑢𝑙𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(247.25pt, 642.58pt, "/𝜏")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(258.65pt, 644.98pt, "𝑖𝑛𝑡𝑒𝑟𝑓𝑎𝑐𝑒")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(299.93pt, 642.58pt, ", where ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(346.15pt, 642.58pt, "𝜏")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(351.67pt, 644.98pt, "𝑏𝑢𝑙𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(371.11pt, 642.58pt, " stand for the bulk diffusion ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 660.22pt, "characteristic time, while ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(206.69pt, 660.22pt, "𝜏")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(212.21pt, 662.62pt, "𝑖𝑛𝑡𝑒𝑟𝑓𝑎𝑐𝑒")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(253.61pt, 660.22pt, " represents characteristic timescales of the interface ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 677.62pt, "equilibration and interfacial mass transfer. The contributions of the bulk and interfacial ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 693.46pt, "transport can be indirectly divided and quantified by performing a broad PA depth-profiling ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 709.3pt, "studies accompanied with a proper mathematical analyses, as shown in [11]. In comparison, a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 725.26pt, "direct access to the surface equilibration kinetics is provided by the time-dependent surface ")
